In many respects, this is the most mouthwatering and unpredictable WSL seas=
on of recent years. At the top end, the title race is wide open from the ou=
tset. The holders, Manchester City, have perhaps the most settled squad and=
 a potent attack spearheaded by the winner of three back-to-back Golden Boo=
ts, Bunny Shaw. However, they will also be juggling their league and FA Cup=
 title defences alongside Champions League football, which they avoided las=
t season through a fourth-place finish in the league the preceding year. Th=
at will be counteracted somewhat by Champions League teams no longer compet=
ing in the rebranded League Cup, which also opens the door to domestic silv=
erware for those outside the top three.

Arsenal and Ren=C3=A9e Slegers have no excuses for a poor season, after two=
 disappointing and costly starts to WSL campaigns were somewhat understanda=
ble due to the tight turnarounds following the major tournament exploits of=
 many players, and Champions League qualifying campaigns in the 2022-23 and=
 2023-24 season. The Gunners have overhauled the squad in the summer and lo=
wered the age profile, adding experienced top-level talent such as Georgia =
Stanway, Ona Batlle, G=C3=A9raldine Reuteler and Selina Cerci.

Chelsea have embarked on their own squad evolution, after a transitional se=
ason where they sought to strike the balance between making changes and ret=
aining that winning spirit synonymous with the side. Some hugely influentia=
l and longstanding players have departed in Millie Bright, who has retired,=
 Guro Reiten, Sam Kerr, Catarina Macario, Johanna Rytting Kaneryd and Niamh=
 Charles.

Melvine Malard, who has reunited with Sonia Bompastor having played under h=
er at OL Lyonnes, has already hit the ground running with two goals from th=
e bench in the team=E2=80=99s
